    rbo:mbid "de9960f8-efc9-4e25-9632-e4e71eb6eaa8" ;
    rbo:publishedOn "2024-04-03T04:41:57+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rbo:rating 5 ;
    rbo:reviewText """Rolling Stone magazine called it the best album of the 80s despite it being released technically in UK in 1979. With an iconic cover of *Paul Simonon* smashing his bass { a nod to Elvis Presley playing his guitar on a certain album }, this album is the band at it's peak. Folks said it wasn't a *punk* thing for the Clash to do, but going against your record company and selling your double LP for the price of a single one is a definitely a very punk thing to do, IMO.\r
\r
The album starts with the clarion call of the title track before settling down into one of the covers, I think. I don't think *Brand New Cadillac* really improves much on the original, but it's doable. There's *Jimmy Jazz* with it's soft setting and *Hateful*, which is a song about drugs. Some Jamaican influence in lyrics is shown on *Rudie Can't Fail* before we ride into *Spanish bombs*. This song compares the Spanish Civil War with basically the bombings that were happening back home then, usually by the IRA. With lyrics referencing the colors 'red' and 'black' which is a reference to communists and anarchists fighting in the Civil War. *Right Profile* is about the Hollywood actor, **Montgomery Clift** before we go into the Mick Jones' sung *Lost in the Supermarket*, which to me is a sense of loneliness in the capitalistic world. *Clampdown* is basically a revolution call with lyrics like "Come over the wall, coz government's to fall". The next song, and one of my favorites is the *Guns of Brixton*. It was partially inspired by the Jimmy Cliff film *The Harder they Come* and coming into a full circle, **Jimmy Cliff** later covered this song as well. Paul Simonon, the bassist, sings this and plays the walking bassline. He, however couldn't play the riff when singing live, so someone else would play the bassline whilst he sung. The next set of songs, I feel, are slightly weaker but *Death or Glory* really shines with Strummer's lyrics. *The Card Cheat*, feels very different instrumentally from the early Clash days and is a testament of how much the band had matured.  I hate *Lover's Rock*, which has cringey lyrics for a Clash song. *Four Horsemen* is a self referential song and *I'm not Down* is an optimistic one. *Revolution Rock* is a cover, but this is an improvement over the original, I feel. *Train In Vain*, as is widely known, was not listed on the original pressings and was kinda a hidden track. But it became a huge hit for the Clash.\r
\r
The band would technically break through in the US with *Combat Rock*, but this album remains their finest."""@en ;
    rbo:reviewUrl "https://critiquebrainz.org/review/de9960f8-efc9-4e25-9632-e4e71eb6eaa8"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    rbo:reviewsAlbum res:Album_8d73e45e-7ca1-3cb4-ae28-6da76196c17c ;
    rbo:writtenBy res:User_6f3bbf71-cd35-4aa4-8e82-de3350738c59 .

